{"kind":"report","text":"Independent P1a review for request #225, fulfilling promise #226.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED for exact source head 35636a0b038f171c4a43d08e881b5b40b5c3cbc6, tree 0120744b2e6be0a883e61d859c5c0bfabf63d256, and all thirteen cited candidate artifacts. No material preflight correctness, evidence-integrity, source-fidelity or proposal-publication finding remains. This verdict approves the compatibility investigation and the fidelity/readiness of its interim proposal. It adopts neither encryption direction and passes none of G1–G5.\n\nScope and authority\nI read governing request #208 (db6d4061e4f63d9476b7e0831a6bc3fe4534f61d), its builder commitment, the exact candidate source/configuration/fixtures/docs/NOTICE, and the relevant pinned upstream source and tests. #208 authorizes this bounded research implementation and proposal together, while requiring a separately reviewed and ratified protocol decision before dependent production work.\nReview execution stayed in /Users/hughpyle/play/noseq-worktrees/crypto-preflight. The thirteen changed paths from landed P0 3321e70 are all within #208's scope; all are included in this verdict. P0 host/Worker source, harness/evidence code, mandatory case profile, tests and CI are byte-for-byte unchanged. No notes, plans, README, Atseq source or other actor's work was altered. I edited no candidate source; generated review variants are ignored artifacts. Exact HEAD remained clean before and after execution. git diff --check passed and all four local links in the changed documentation resolve.\n\nActual independent reruns\n- npm ci: passed using the exact Node/npm and lockfile; all new root packages are dev-only exact pins, and the locked graph uses registry URLs with integrity hashes.\n- npm run check: passed, including the dedicated crypto-preflight test configuration and all existing P0 target typechecks.\n- npm run test:harness: the unchanged 8 Node, 2 workerd and 2 actual Chromium page/dedicated-worker cases passed. Reviewer P0 run: artifacts/harness/86d59366-d6e4-4ba5-9f8b-8469706a07c3. evidence.json SHA-256: 6df58e67c98814e636ef76ca30cbcb460c12e46cb76b6ca997dcce501232fa5d.\n- npm run build: client build and local Wrangler dry-run passed; no bindings or deployment.\n- npm run probe:crypto-preflight: frozen upstream dependency installation, explicit ts-mls build, candidate public-export build, all 26 upstream cases and all 7 Noseq preflight cases passed. The final CLI reported Candidate INCOMPATIBLE; G1–G5 UNPASSED, as required.\nReviewer preflight run: artifacts/crypto-preflight/runs/1ba511d2-8784-41f4-876c-3a4ef62d1061. Its evidence.json SHA-256 is 84cd064c043e30663243ed49241f3fd6696e731116c83afa0c9748f8d3302c21. Source/lock/profile/runtime/raw-report identities and exclusive process outcome records are retained there. Noseq lockfile SHA-256: 765e5894ecf149c3f270a8775f662e53d5d5ef0ea029db0b7c066dee097d2238; preflight profile SHA-256: a3616665f693c81cb134c2d7cab8d17cf135982175715171bf68799a79c11258.\n\nIndependent public-verifier rejection probes\nUsing retained copies of my run, npm run verify:crypto-preflight accepted one valid record and rejected sixteen corrupted/incomplete variants: claiming G1 passed, erasing gates, claiming compatibility, missing suite, missing case, duplicate case, skipped case, failed process, changed Noseq source, changed upstream revision, changed crypto package, changed required list, missing raw report, wrong raw hash, raw-case disagreement with a recomputed hash, and failed raw aggregate with a recomputed hash.\nThe test:crypto-feasibility and gate:protocol commands separately exited 1 with stage not implemented. All nineteen CLI outcomes matched expectation.\nRecords and process logs are retained at artifacts/crypto-preflight/reviewer-negative/3e655fd3-7dad-442d-86e0-7ccdff7c2187; summary.json SHA-256: b7d37920909ddf5e50b285822fae1cf577571937f2342a7345a861bb91889911.\nI inspected the retained wrong-upstream failure c09e19d2-22dd-47c4-9955-096ba73be6ca: it records this exact Noseq source, intended input pins/hashes, the actual wrong candidate commit in the rejection diagnostic, null accepted context and no successful evidence. The cache is now clean at the required pin. Existing successful and failed records remain distinct; no test result was replaced to manufacture a pass.\n\nSource and crypto-observation fidelity\nThe exact source checks pass for Marmot 4a2bc65f8db5866cec3b2a127dedb37818eaf207, marmot-ts 2f60dbb27d284f617ad873ccda568c0f2f07aa79, and its actual Git submodule entry ts-mls 2ca5c43b77241245ef41a5dd834f151674877c2d. Origins, clean trees, selected fixture/source hashes, root upstream frozen pnpm lock 9cf5049960026b335885651f74002fb527785cad64e5f1595ab11a3a7d424fd5, pnpm 10.34.5, direct crypto package versions and the separate upstream Vitest 3.2.4/TypeScript 6.0.3 tools are checked. The complete transitive graph is pinned by that lock; documentation accurately labels the smaller displayed version list as the direct surface.\nThe dev-only v2 fixture matches the published canonical signing event, event ID, signature and 104-byte proof. Changed signed fields and signature corruption fail cryptographic verification. The executed built /core API uses the legacy 0xf2f1/version-1/135-byte proof for the selected key, rejects the supplied v2 bytes, and produces a different signing digest whose valid signature only authenticates the v1 request. This is a genuine incompatibility observation, not a full current-protocol validator. Carrier negotiation, duplicate components, key/timestamp validity, signer-response substitution, membership authority and external-signer capabilities remain explicitly untested. [Pinned v2 component](https://github.com/marmot-protocol/marmot/blob/4a2bc65f8db5866cec3b2a127dedb37818eaf207/app-components/account-identity-proof-v2.md), [pinned v1 source](https://github.com/marmot-protocol/marmot-ts/blob/2f60dbb27d284f617ad873ccda568c0f2f07aa79/src/core/account-identity-proof.ts).\nThe current authorization-proof template accommodates normal signed Nostr events. The document correctly confines arbitrary-digest/raw-Schnorr requirements to the legacy API; it does not claim NIP-07 or NIP-46 compatibility was exercised. [Pinned authorization proofs](https://github.com/marmot-protocol/marmot/blob/4a2bc65f8db5866cec3b2a127dedb37818eaf207/foundation/authorization-proofs.md).\nI traced the actual unmodified commit-race fixture, including accepted losing-branch payload at lines 581–592 and exact invalidation identity/payload assertion at 594–627, plus the two rewind persistence cases. They use real MLS commits, encryption/decryption and the selected default WebCrypto/Noble/HPKE provider. Their unsafe test authentication, synthetic credentials, minimal signer, mock network and in-memory stores are real limitations and are preserved in the report. Recreating objects over retained serialized memory is not filesystem crash recovery, secure admission, live relay behavior, a signed Noseq prefix or G2 proof.\nThe current pinned convergence contract also withdraws abandoned effects but includes bounded frozen-input passes and observer-atomic application. Documentation does not misrepresent the older 26-case corpus as current Marmot conformance or proof that every possible finality adapter is impossible. The source-level optional v1 KeyPackage proof is not presented as satisfying mandatory v2 presence.\n\nProposal assessment\nThe comparison is faithful and sufficiently concrete for a separate protocol decision:\n1. Preserving Marmot requires an actually compatible component/transport/admission implementation and a demonstrated finality adapter that honors valid convergence input. The current pair's production use stops.\n2. The preferred next investigation is explicitly a different, versioned Noseq MLS/Nostr binding, with common signed order, first valid Commit, pending sender state until ordered self-echo, and coupled Welcome acceptance. It does not inherit Marmot interoperability or turn its existing eager MarmotGroup API into that adapter by assertion.\nRFC 9750 section 5.2.1 describes this delivery-service ordering model, including delayed self-application. RFC 9420 section 14 requires conflict resolution, separates Commit generation from state mutation and delays Welcome delivery until acceptance. Those sources motivate the experiment, not an implementation security claim. [RFC 9750 §5.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9750.html#section-5.2.1), [RFC 9420 §14](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9420.html#section-14).\nThe proposal names changed branch-selection/withdrawal and wire/interoperability semantics, requires a suitable provisional-state MLS API, and lists actual next vectors for winning/losing self-echo, competing commits, delayed Welcome, stale inputs, replay and crash boundaries. It preserves server opacity, explicit metadata exposure, authenticated account/leaf and independent action proof, owner-controlled admission boundaries, future-epoch exclusion, archive/recovery closure and custody, stable interpreted prefixes, observed-fork refusal and common invalid/unavailable-input policy. No callback freeze, indefinite wait, arbitrary rejection of valid Marmot input or unrotated shared key is accepted as a G1 fix. Full signer/history/admission/wire/relay, cross-runtime crypto and adversary-by-asset gates remain required and unpassed.\n\nAttribution, hosted claim and limits\nNOTICE retains the original repository copyright and the complete exact MIT texts of all three identified upstream repositories. Only public synthetic fixture material is committed; upstream working sources/licenses remain fetched and ignored.\nThe P0 hosted-result update is grounded in effective, ratified assertion #210 (91a8b1d4f1d1ddcd7039287f0859b2f8bcae842f), naming source 3321e70, GitHub run 34064545219 and its independently verified P0 evidence. The document correctly limits that assertion to P0. This review independently reran P1a on macOS arm64/Node 26.8.1; no hosted P1 crypto, cross-runtime crypto, production deployment, independent package-tarball equivalence or full cryptographic audit was performed.\nEvidence controls establish reproducibility and review consistency, not hostile-source attestation, as stated. Publication approval under #208 does not authorize production use of either crypto direction. Builder remains responsible for requester ratification and coordinated guarded landing; I did not self-ratify, merge or push.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:1f0c59b5b43042cf0fbdc4848533d351a06ca4e4","head":"35636a0b038f171c4a43d08e881b5b40b5c3cbc6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:853d71ea1d667f669ea986172db76b619446ddf3\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:853d71ea1d667f669ea986172db76b619446ddf3","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}